From de3e0ef46d287bb5043801cbb9c93f82d3f87567 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 15:24:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: size process cores by the guest's memory, grow the
 buffer in chunks, report why a core is cut

A process core was limited to 64 MiB and 5 s by constants in four places, and
its buffer was one allocation of that size made when the core was armed. The
limit is now derived from the guest: half of RAM less 128 MiB, clamped to
64 MiB .. 1 GiB, with a pool of twice that and a deadline of up to 60 s.

The buffer is a table of 8 MiB chunks allocated as the sink writes, so a budget
costs nothing until a process really is that large and running out of memory
ends the core as a partial instead of failing one big allocation. An exit core
still takes its first 64 MiB when it is armed. The extent table scales with the
budget; the manual path sizes its pinned-page list by it. The ELF header, program
headers and notes are read from the first chunk and the parsers fail closed.

The sink is now the only budget enforcer (the writer is given no limit), so the
status errno means one thing: EFBIG the budget, ENOMEM no chunk, E2BIG the
extent table, EOVERFLOW a logical extent past 16 GiB. CORE_CAPS (0x1066) reports
the limits and CORE_STATUS takes a STORED header flag that appends the stored
byte count. Both are additive: an earlier guest answers CORE_CAPS with
EOPNOTSUPP and every earlier request is accepted unchanged.
---
 kernel/antinomie/control.c      |  11 +-
 kernel/antinomie/control_wire.h |  38 ++++-
 kernel/antinomie/manual_core.c  |  15 +-
 kernel/antinomie/process_core.c | 267 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 kernel/antinomie/process_core.h |   5 +
 5 files changed, 274 insertions(+), 62 deletions(-)

